A new teacher stands outside a classroom that is not yet hers.
She has done this before, in another school. She remembers what the first week was: learning which form goes where, which colleague to ask, which list is the current one and which is the one everybody stopped updating in March. Six weeks of learning the building before she could begin teaching in it. The children waited. Not visibly. But they waited.
Not here.
She arrived on Monday and the school already knew she was coming. Her classes were hers. Her timetable was hers. The roll in front of her carries thirty names, and beside each one, everything the school has ever understood about that child — what they are strong in, what they are quiet about, who to call, what happened last term that someone thought worth writing down.
She has taught none of them. She knows all of them.
So the first lesson is a lesson. Not an introduction to a system. Not a week of catching up to a school that was already running without her.
By Friday no one can tell she is new. The children can't. Because to a child, a teacher who knows your name on the first morning has always been there.
This is what an institution is: what remains when the people change. A school that must be learned by each new arrival is not an institution. It is a memory, distributed across whoever has not yet left.
The new teacher belongs from the first morning with Edisapp, An Intelligent Operating System for Modern Schools.

